1
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:06,967
Welcome to the Feelandnote Library Tour,

2
00:00:06,967 --> 00:00:14,017
where we open the personal libraries of remarkable figures and explore the works they loved, and why.

3
00:00:15,517 --> 00:00:18,533
Today's guest is Alexander the Great.

4
00:00:21,033 --> 00:00:25,883
He who conquers his fear shall conquer the world itself.

5
00:00:30,383 --> 00:00:32,517
Alexander III of Macedon,

6
00:00:32,517 --> 00:00:34,483
known as Alexander the Great,

7
00:00:34,483 --> 00:00:40,817
was king of the ancient Macedonian empire and the most formidable conqueror in recorded history.

8
00:00:40,817 --> 00:00:42,900
Ascending the throne at twenty,

9
00:00:42,900 --> 00:00:46,950
he forged a vast empire stretching from Greece to India,

10
00:00:46,950 --> 00:00:52,433
and his spread of Hellenistic culture left an indelible mark on civilization.

11
00:00:54,933 --> 00:00:57,167
I am Alexander, King of Macedon.

12
00:00:57,167 --> 00:01:00,933
The Iliad awakened the spirit of the hero within me,

13
00:01:00,933 --> 00:01:05,300
and the wisdom of history gave me the blade to pierce the heart of empires.

14
00:01:05,300 --> 00:01:09,200
On blood-soaked battlefields I recited tragedy,

15
00:01:09,200 --> 00:01:11,750
and before poets I sheathed my sword.

16
00:01:11,750 --> 00:01:14,900
Through the endless exhaustion of campaign,

17
00:01:14,900 --> 00:01:18,533
literature was the supply line of my soul.

18
00:01:23,533 --> 00:01:27,467
The Iliad, Homer, 8th century BC

19
00:01:32,533 --> 00:01:36,250
An epic poem by Homer from the 8th century BC.

20
00:01:36,250 --> 00:01:41,833
It chronicles the final year of the ten-year war between the Greek coalition and Troy.

21
00:01:41,833 --> 00:01:47,700
When the greatest warrior Achilles withdraws from battle after a feud with Commander Agamemnon,

22
00:01:47,700 --> 00:01:54,150
the death of his companion Patroclus drives him back to the field in a storm of rage and grief.

23
00:01:54,150 --> 00:01:57,250
It is the wellspring of Western literature,

24
00:01:57,250 --> 00:02:00,133
a song of war's glory and tragedy,

25
00:02:00,133 --> 00:02:01,950
of honor and fate.

26
00:02:09,350 --> 00:02:12,550
Alexander the Great slept with a copy of the Iliad,

27
00:02:12,550 --> 00:02:15,383
personally annotated by his tutor Aristotle,

28
00:02:15,383 --> 00:02:18,083
tucked beneath his pillow alongside a dagger.

29
00:02:18,083 --> 00:02:21,633
He called it a treasury of all military virtue.

30
00:02:21,633 --> 00:02:24,317
After launching his Persian campaign,

31
00:02:24,317 --> 00:02:26,317
his first detour was to Troy,

32
00:02:26,317 --> 00:02:29,083
where he laid a wreath at the tomb of Achilles.

33
00:02:33,583 --> 00:02:35,083
"Achilles was truly blessed,

34
00:02:35,083 --> 00:02:37,400
in life he had a loyal companion,

35
00:02:37,400 --> 00:02:40,867
and in death he found a great poet to sing of his deeds."

36
00:02:43,033 --> 00:02:44,617
After conquering Persia,

37
00:02:44,617 --> 00:02:49,517
Alexander stored this manuscript in a jeweled casket seized from Darius III.

38
00:02:49,517 --> 00:02:55,083
He chose the Iliad as the one treasure worthy of the most precious chest in the world.

39
00:02:59,583 --> 00:03:03,633
The Odyssey, Homer, 8th century BC

40
00:03:08,700 --> 00:03:10,767
The sequel to the Iliad.

41
00:03:10,767 --> 00:03:12,633
After the fall of Troy,

42
00:03:12,633 --> 00:03:18,100
the cunning hero Odysseus embarks on a decade-long voyage home to Ithaca.

43
00:03:18,100 --> 00:03:24,133
From the cave of the Cyclops to the song of the Sirens and a descent into the underworld,

44
00:03:24,133 --> 00:03:28,983
this mythic odyssey celebrates human wisdom, endurance,

45
00:03:28,983 --> 00:03:31,317
and the meaning of homecoming.

46
00:03:38,717 --> 00:03:42,117
When Alexander set out to found a new city in Egypt,

47
00:03:42,117 --> 00:03:46,800
legend holds that Homer appeared to him in a dream and recited a passage from the Odyssey,

48
00:03:46,800 --> 00:03:51,100
inspiring him to choose the site of Alexandria.

49
00:03:51,100 --> 00:03:56,733
Alexander praised Homer's poetry for guiding him to the perfect location for his city.

50
00:04:01,233 --> 00:04:05,050
"Homer's verse led me to the perfect site for my city."

51
00:04:09,550 --> 00:04:14,167
Cyropaedia, Xenophon, c. 370 BC

52
00:04:19,233 --> 00:04:20,533
Written by Xenophon,

53
00:04:20,533 --> 00:04:23,533
this work chronicles the life of Cyrus the Great,

54
00:04:23,533 --> 00:04:25,367
founder of the Persian Empire.

55
00:04:25,367 --> 00:04:28,400
More political philosophy than strict history,

56
00:04:28,400 --> 00:04:34,317
it projects the virtues of an ideal ruler onto the figure of Cyrus, his valor in battle,

57
00:04:34,317 --> 00:04:35,850
his wisdom in governance,

58
00:04:35,850 --> 00:04:37,900
his generosity toward soldiers,

59
00:04:37,900 --> 00:04:40,200
and his firmness against enemies.

60
00:04:47,617 --> 00:04:52,433
Alexander read this book from childhood and deeply admired Cyrus the Great.

61
00:04:52,433 --> 00:04:56,750
That admiration revealed itself after his conquest of Persia.

62
00:04:56,750 --> 00:05:03,783
When Alexander visited the tomb of Cyrus at Pasargadae and discovered that guards had desecrated the burial chamber,

63
00:05:03,783 --> 00:05:09,183
he ordered their immediate execution and commanded the tomb's full restoration.

64
00:05:13,700 --> 00:05:18,600
Anabasis, Xenophon, c. 370 BC

65
00:05:23,667 --> 00:05:29,967
A firsthand account of ten thousand Greek mercenaries who joined the rebellion of Cyrus the Younger in 401 BC.

66
00:05:29,967 --> 00:05:34,000
After marching deep into Persia and losing their commanders to treachery,

67
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:36,600
they fought their way back through hostile territory.

68
00:05:36,600 --> 00:05:38,750
Written by Xenophon himself,

69
00:05:38,750 --> 00:05:42,417
it is rich in tactical detail, terrain, logistics,

70
00:05:42,417 --> 00:05:43,917
and battlefield strategy.

71
00:05:51,317 --> 00:05:56,400
This book served as Alexander's practical military guide for his Persian campaign.

72
00:05:56,400 --> 00:06:00,217
No Macedonian had ever penetrated so deep into Asia,

73
00:06:00,217 --> 00:06:02,433
only Xenophon had mapped the route.

74
00:06:02,433 --> 00:06:05,800
Alexander studied Mesopotamian geography,

75
00:06:05,800 --> 00:06:07,800
Persian military tactics,

76
00:06:07,800 --> 00:06:11,933
and logistics in hostile territory from these very pages.

77
00:06:16,433 --> 00:06:22,017
Ancient Greek Lyric Poetry, Pindar et al., 5th century BC

78
00:06:27,083 --> 00:06:29,883
An anthology of ancient Greek lyric poets,

79
00:06:29,883 --> 00:06:31,917
including Pindar's victory odes,

80
00:06:31,917 --> 00:06:33,500
Sappho's love poems,

81
00:06:33,500 --> 00:06:35,350
and Alcaeus's war songs.

82
00:06:35,350 --> 00:06:37,000
Pindar's odes,

83
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:38,867
composed for Olympic champions,

84
00:06:38,867 --> 00:06:42,000
are choral hymns celebrating heroic ideals,

85
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:44,900
the pinnacle of classical Greek literature.

86
00:06:52,300 --> 00:06:53,067
In 335 BC,

87
00:06:53,067 --> 00:06:58,183
when Alexander crushed the Theban revolt and razed the city,

88
00:06:58,183 --> 00:07:05,083
he gave a single order of preservation: spare the temples, the house of Pindar, and his descendants.

89
00:07:05,083 --> 00:07:09,317
Pindar had once written odes praising Alexander's ancestors.

90
00:07:09,317 --> 00:07:11,817
He burned a city to the ground,

91
00:07:11,817 --> 00:07:13,983
yet spared a poet's home.

92
00:07:18,500 --> 00:07:23,517
The Tragedies of Aeschylus, Aeschylus, 472 BC

93
00:07:28,583 --> 00:07:32,050
The collected works of Aeschylus, the father of tragedy.

94
00:07:32,050 --> 00:07:34,600
Among his seven surviving plays,

95
00:07:34,600 --> 00:07:36,117
The Persians stands out,

96
00:07:36,117 --> 00:07:42,350
the only extant war tragedy depicting the defeat at Salamis from the Persian court's perspective.

97
00:07:49,767 --> 00:07:52,567
When books ran short during the Persian campaign,

98
00:07:52,567 --> 00:07:56,567
Alexander requested fresh supplies from his treasurer Harpalus,

99
00:07:56,567 --> 00:07:58,250
and Aeschylus was among them.

100
00:07:58,250 --> 00:08:06,017
A play about Greece's victory over Persia must have held special resonance for a man who was marching to conquer Persia himself.

101
00:08:10,517 --> 00:08:15,733
The Tragedies of Sophocles, Sophocles, 5th century BC

102
00:08:20,800 --> 00:08:24,600
Tragedies exploring the collision between fate and human will.

103
00:08:24,600 --> 00:08:28,033
Oedipus Rex, Antigone, Electra,

104
00:08:28,033 --> 00:08:33,617
Sophocles paints the heroic defiance of mortals confronting inescapable destiny.

105
00:08:33,617 --> 00:08:37,167
He is hailed as the master of tragic irony.

106
00:08:44,583 --> 00:08:49,600
Another work Alexander received from his treasurer Harpalus during the inland campaign.

107
00:08:49,600 --> 00:08:53,383
He had complete sets of tragedies shipped to the front lines,

108
00:08:53,383 --> 00:08:56,783
proof that he regarded literature as essential supply,

109
00:08:56,783 --> 00:08:59,517
as vital as grain or weapons.

110
00:09:04,017 --> 00:09:09,750
The Tragedies of Euripides, Euripides, 5th century BC

111
00:09:14,817 --> 00:09:16,367
The works of Euripides,

112
00:09:16,367 --> 00:09:22,233
celebrated for his penetrating psychological insight and vivid portrayal of women. Medea,

113
00:09:22,233 --> 00:09:24,117
The Bacchae,

114
00:09:24,117 --> 00:09:28,150
and other plays reinterpret traditional myths through a profoundly human lens.

115
00:09:28,150 --> 00:09:31,950
He was the most popular tragedian of Alexander's era.

116
00:09:39,350 --> 00:09:41,983
Also shipped from Harpalus during the campaign.

117
00:09:41,983 --> 00:09:45,067
As the most popular tragedian of the age,

118
00:09:45,067 --> 00:09:48,333
Euripides offered a human perspective on ancient myth,

119
00:09:48,333 --> 00:09:52,917
insight that would have served a king seeking to understand the people he ruled.

120
00:09:59,433 --> 00:10:04,200
Those were the eight books that accompanied Alexander the Great across his battlefields.

121
00:10:04,200 --> 00:10:11,700
Curious about the deeper story of the conqueror who wielded a sword in one hand and a book in the other, twenty-four centuries ago?

122
00:10:11,700 --> 00:10:13,800
Discover more on Feel & Note.
